 {
  "source": "Barclays",
  "title": "2026 Global Energy Transition Barometer: The Next Transition Trade",
  "type": "strategy",
  "priority": "medium",
  "published": "2026-09-08",
  "insights": [
   "Global energy transition momentum is strengthening across all regions, but regional pathways are diverging significantly due to differences in power demand, infrastructure, and market structure.",
   "The 'next transition trade' is shifting from renewable generation to enabling infrastructure, specifically transmission, distribution, battery storage, and firm power sources like nuclear and geothermal.",
   "China leads in transition momentum but faces significant grid absorption and overcapacity challenges in solar and battery manufacturing.",
   "Europe's transition is supply-led, creating a 'green generation discount' but increasing system balancing friction, which elevates the value of flexibility, interconnectors, and energy management.",
   "North America's transition is more incremental, with the primary investment driver shifting to 'speed-to-power' solutions to meet AI-related electricity demand.",
   "Grid investment is failing to keep pace with renewable capacity growth globally, creating a 'grid investment gap' that serves as a primary source of transition friction.",
   "The investment opportunity set is broadening to include regulated utilities, electrical equipment manufacturers, and industrial electrification solutions."
  ],
  "sector_views": [
   {
    "sector": "Utilities",
    "stance": "overweight",
    "rationale": "Regulated utilities are key beneficiaries of the need for grid expansion and reliable power delivery to meet rising load growth."
   },
   {
    "sector": "Electrical Equipment",
    "stance": "overweight",
    "rationale": "High demand for transformers, switchgear, and cables to address grid bottlenecks and AI-related power needs."
   },
   {
    "sector": "Solar/Battery Manufacturers",
    "stance": "neutral",
