 {
  "source": "Barclays",
  "title": "The global energy race: Energy in the age of fragmentation",
  "type": "strategy",
  "priority": "medium",
  "published": "2026-09-08",
  "insights": [
   "Global energy demand is entering an era of 'addition' rather than 'substitution', with simultaneous growth in oil, gas, and renewables driven by AI, electrification, and energy security.",
   "Annual energy investment requirements are estimated to reach $3.6 trillion by 2027, creating a massive capital-allocation cycle that exceeds planned AI infrastructure spending.",
   "Geopolitical fragmentation is forcing a shift from cost-optimized to resilience-optimized energy systems, necessitating redundant infrastructure and regionalized supply chains.",
   "Data centers are a new structural source of baseload electricity demand, growing at 25-30% annually, which is straining grid capacity and permitting timelines.",
   "The energy sector is structurally tight with low inventories and constrained supply, creating upside risks to commodity prices and persistent inflation that markets may be underestimating.",
   "US upstream production is maturing, shifting the value proposition from unconstrained growth to scarcity value and strategic importance of secure molecules.",
   "European utilities are entering an investment super-cycle focused on grids, baseload power, and energy security, with a shift toward state-supported funding models.",
   "The US power grid faces a potential supply/demand gap of 50 GW by 2028, with permitting bottlenecks and long lead times for equipment acting as binding constraints.",
   "Energy services companies are experiencing their best setup in 20 years, benefiting from the need to maintain aging infrastructure while simultaneously enabling new energy sources.",
   "The US dollar is supported by 'energy exceptionalism' and data sovereignty, with energy-exporting nations and commodity-rich economies gaining relative competitiveness."
